# PERROR(3)

## NAME

perror, strerror, sys_errlist, sys_nerr - system error messages

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void perror(const char *s);

#include <string.h>

char *strerror(int errnum);

extern int sys_nerr;
extern char *sys_errlist[];
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Perror* produces a short error message on the standard error file describing the last error
encountered during a call to the system from a C program. First the argument string *s* is
printed, then a colon, then the message and a new-line. Most usefully, the argument string is
the name of the program which incurred the error. The error number is taken from the external
variable *errno* (see intro(2)), which is set when errors occur but not cleared when
non-erroneous calls are made.

To simplify variant formatting of messages, the vector of message strings *sys_errlist* is
provided; *errno* can be used as an index in this table to get the message string without the
newline. *Sys_nerr* is the number of messages provided for in the table; it should be checked
because new error codes may be added to the system before they are added to the table.

**Note:** *strerror* is C11's name for that lookup and is the one to use - it does the
*sys_nerr* check for the caller and answers `Unknown error` for anything outside the table,
including a negative number. *Perror* is written in terms of it, so the two can never name the
same *errno* differently. v7 had no *strerror* at all; a program that wanted the text indexed
*sys_errlist* itself, unchecked. The string it returns must not be modified.

## SEE ALSO

intro(2)

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/perror.c, strerror.c, errlst.c, include/errno.h

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The table is 36 entries, and no more is possible

The value the **$77** gate leaves in r14 is always one of the numbers <errno.h> defines: the
kernel assigns from its own copy of the list, and b6sim folds the host's numbering onto the
same set. Nothing else can arrive from a system call, so a *sys_nerr* check can never fail on a
kernel error - which is not to say it should be dropped.

**The last three are not kernel codes at all.** 33 and 34 - EDOM and ERANGE - are set by libm,
and 35 - EILSEQ - belongs to the multibyte conversions. They are in the table because C11 sec
7.5 requires all three to exist and *perror* and *strerror* must be able to name them. Nothing
sets EILSEQ yet.

### An array of char \* is one word per element

A fat pointer fits in a word, marker bit and byte offset and all, so this table costs 36 words
and not 36 pointers' worth of anything larger.

### errno cannot be read at the call site in C

r14 is caller-saved and the compiler loads it with the negative argument count before every
call, so any C statement between the system call and the test would have destroyed it. Each
stub tests r14 itself and branches to a shared *cerror,* which stores the value into *errno*
and returns -1. **That is the whole reason lib/libc/sys is assembly and not C,** and it is why
a stub that forgets the branch still returns -1 - the gate puts it there - with only *errno* to
give it away.
